  "prompt": "Modify your `hop_counts` function to record how many times the container operation that removes an element is executed. Run the function on a moderately sized graph (e.g., 10,000 vertices and a chain of edges) using the current list‑based `stack` and note the total count. Then replace the list with `collections.deque` (use `popleft()` instead of `pop()`) and run the same graph again. Compare the two counts and the runtime.",
  "trace_question": "What difference do you observe in the number of removal operations (and overall execution time) when using a list versus a deque for the frontier container?",
  "expected_insight": "You will see that the list version performs many more element‑shifts (or pop operations) and runs noticeably slower, revealing that a plain list is not the appropriate container for a FIFO queue in BFS; a deque provides O(1) removals and yields the expected linear‑time behavior."
